<section>
  ## Small edits, one at a time

  <code lang=typescript id=g1>
    <frame>
      if (count > 10) {
        alert("too many items: " + count);
      }
    </frame>
    <frame>
      if (count >= 10) {
        alert("too many items: " + count);
      }
    </frame>
    <frame>
      if (count >= limit) {
        alert("too many items: " + count);
      }
    </frame>
    <frame>
      if (count >= limit) {
        alert(`too many items: ${count}`);
      }
    </frame>
    <frame>
      if (count >= limit) {
        notify(`too many items: ${count}`);
      }
    </frame>
  </code>
</section>

<section>
  ## Reorder arguments

  <code lang=typescript id=g2>
    <frame>
      const point = makePoint(y, x, label, color);
    </frame>
    <frame>
      const point = makePoint(x, y, label, color);
    </frame>
    <frame>
      const point = makePoint(x, y, color, label);
    </frame>
  </code>
</section>

<section>
  ## if / else to a ternary

  <code lang=typescript>
    <frame>
      let label;
      if (count === 1) {
        label = "item";
      } else {
        label = "items";
      }
    </frame>
    <frame>
      const label = count === 1 ? "item" : "items";
    </frame>
  </code>
</section>

<section>
  ## Loop to method chain

  <code lang=typescript>
    <frame>
      const names = [];
      for (const user of users) {
        if (user.active) {
          names.push(user.name);
        }
      }
    </frame>
    <frame>
      const names = users
        .filter((user) => user.active)
        .map((user) => user.name);
    </frame>
  </code>
</section>

<notes>
  Each edit changes exactly one token or a tiny region; everything else must stay still.
</notes>
